  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simple energy schematic for anion intercalation, showing how a solvated anion moves from solution into a host material. On the left, the anion is surrounded by solvent molecules, representing a “solvation anion” state at a lower energy level. In the middle, the same anion is shown alone as an “isolated anion,” at a higher energy, indicating that energy must be supplied to strip away the solvent shell. This upward step is labeled as the desolvation energy, emphasizing that desolvation is an energetic cost.\n\nOn the right, the isolated anion is inserted into a crystalline host lattice, labeled as the intercalant. Here the energy drops again, shown by a downward arrow labeled intercalation energy. The host is drawn as a repeating blue framework with gold spheres occupying sites within it, conveying that the anion becomes stabilized inside the solid structure.\n\nOverall, the graphic explains the balance of two key energetic contributions during intercalation: first the penalty for removing solvation, and then the stabilization gained when the anion enters the intercalation host. The figure highlights that the net process depends on these competing energy changes.",
